      <!-- Q&A Library -->
      <section class="tb-library">
        <div class="tb-library-intro">
          <h2>Common Questions &amp; Answers</h2>
          <p>Browse 31 real compliance and business questions — or click <em>Ask Tradie Brain</em> to send any question straight to the chat above.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Category 1: CER Audits & Compliance -->
        <div class="lib-category">
          <button class="lib-cat-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="lib-cat-1">
            <span class="lib-cat-icon">📋</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-title">CER Audits &amp; Compliance</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-count">6 Q&amp;As</span>
            <svg class="lib-cat-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="lib-cat-body" id="lib-cat-1">

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-1-1" data-prompt="Give me a full pre-CER audit checklist for a solar installer">
                What documents do I need to have ready for a CER audit?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-1-1">
                <p>For a CER audit you need to produce, for every installation audited:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Signed customer consent form</strong> — must be signed before STC assignment, not after</li>
                  <li><strong>Assignment form (RESA)</strong> — Renewable Energy System Assignment form signed by the customer assigning STCs to your business</li>
                  <li><strong>Installation declaration</strong> — installer's declaration that the system meets AS/NZS 5033</li>
                  <li><strong>Compliant wiring diagram</strong> — site-specific, shows array layout, inverter, protection devices, labelling</li>
                  <li><strong>Inverter commissioning record</strong> — including export limit confirmation where applicable</li>
                  <li><strong>Battery commissioning record</strong> (if applicable) — including AS/NZS 5139 clearance documentation</li>
                  <li><strong>Electrical safety certificate</strong> — CES (VIC) or CCEW (NSW) or equivalent for your state</li>
                  <li><strong>Network approval / DNSP notification</strong> — evidence of submission and approval for grid-connected systems</li>
                  <li><strong>Photos</strong> — array, inverter label, main switchboard before and after, labelling, battery installation</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Common CER audit triggers include: STC claim volume spikes, customer complaints, installation quality flags from retailers, random selection, or state-regulator referrals.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="Give me a full pre-CER audit checklist for a solar installer">⚡ Ask Tradie Brain for the full checklist</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-1-2" data-prompt="What are the most common reasons solar installers fail a CER audit?">
                What are the most common CER audit failures for solar installers?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-1-2">
                <p>The most common CER audit failures are:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Consent forms signed after STC assignment</strong> — the consent must precede the claim</li>
                  <li><strong>Generic wiring diagrams</strong> — site-specific diagrams are required; stock templates fail</li>
                  <li><strong>Missing or incorrect labelling</strong> — AS/NZS 5033 requires labels at array, inverter, switchboard, and isolation points</li>
                  <li><strong>Installer declaration not retained</strong> — must be kept and produced on request</li>
                  <li><strong>STC quantity miscalculation</strong> — oversizing claimed STCs vs actual installed capacity</li>
                  <li><strong>Subcontractor compliance gaps</strong> — you are responsible for the compliance of subbies you use</li>
                  <li><strong>No evidence of network approval</strong> — DNSP application confirmation must be on file</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What are the most common reasons solar installers fail a CER audit?">⚡ Ask Tradie Brain about audit failures</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-1-3" data-prompt="I've received a CER compliance notice. What should I do first?">
                I've received a CER compliance notice — what should I do?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-1-3">
                <p>Don't panic — a compliance notice is not an automatic penalty. Your immediate steps:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Read carefully and note the response deadline</strong> — typically 14–28 days; missing it escalates the matter</li>
                  <li><strong>Pull all documentation for the flagged installations</strong> — gather every record listed above</li>
                  <li><strong>Identify gaps before responding</strong> — do not submit a response until you know where your records stand</li>
                  <li><strong>Respond factually</strong> — explain what happened, what you have, and what remediation action you've taken or will take</li>
                  <li><strong>Offer a corrective action plan</strong> — the CER responds well to installers who demonstrate they've fixed the root cause</li>
                </ul>
                <p>If you cannot locate records for specific installations, check whether they can be reconstructed from network approval emails, inverter portal data, or retailer records. Ask Tradie Brain for help drafting your response.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="I've received a CER compliance notice. What should I do first?">⚡ Ask Tradie Brain for guidance</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-1-4" data-prompt="What is the CCEW 7-day rule in NSW and what work triggers it for a solar installer?">
                What is the CCEW 7-day rule in NSW?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-1-4">
                <p>Under the NSW Home Building Act 1989, a licensed electrician must lodge a <strong>Certificate of Compliance Electrical Work (CCEW)</strong> within <strong>7 days of completing</strong> any electrical installation, alteration, addition, or repair work.</p>
                <p>For solar/battery installers in NSW, a CCEW is required for:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Every new solar PV installation connected to the grid or meter board</li>
                  <li>Every battery storage system installation</li>
                  <li>Switchboard upgrades or modifications during an install</li>
                  <li>EV charger installations</li>
                </ul>
                <p>The CCEW is lodged through the NSW Fair Trading portal. Non-lodgement or late lodgement can attract fines and may be flagged to the CER.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What is the CCEW 7-day rule in NSW and what work triggers it for a solar installer?">⚡ Ask about CCEW in NSW</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-1-5" data-prompt="What are the Certificate of Electrical Safety (CES) requirements in Victoria for solar installers?">
                What are the CES requirements for solar installers in Victoria?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-1-5">
                <p>In Victoria, a <strong>Certificate of Electrical Safety (CES)</strong> must be issued under the Electrical Safety Act 1998 for all electrical installation work, including solar PV and battery installations.</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>The CES must be issued by the <strong>Licensed Electrical Inspector (LEI)</strong> or the installing contractor where permitted</li>
                  <li>It must be issued within <strong>7 days of completion</strong> of the work</li>
                  <li>A copy must be provided to the property owner or occupier</li>
                  <li>Records must be retained for a minimum period</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Solar CES requirements in VIC align with AS/NZS 5033 compliance. Failure to issue a CES is a breach of the Electrical Safety Act and can affect your CER standing.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What are the Certificate of Electrical Safety (CES) requirements in Victoria for solar installers?">⚡ Ask about CES Victoria</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-1-6" data-prompt="How do I calculate and claim STCs for a solar installation in Australia?">
                How do I calculate and claim STCs for a solar installation?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-1-6">
                <p>STCs (Small-scale Technology Certificates) are calculated based on the system's expected generation over its deeming period (up to 2030), multiplied by the postcode zone factor.</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Formula:</strong> System capacity (kW) × deeming years remaining × zone multiplier</li>
                  <li>Use the CER's STC calculator at <strong>rec-registry.gov.au</strong> for exact figures</li>
                  <li>The customer signs an RESA form to assign STCs to you or your retailer</li>
                  <li>STCs must be created within <strong>12 months</strong> of installation</li>
                  <li>Only CEC-accredited installers can create STCs</li>
                  <li>Common errors: claiming for batteries separately (batteries don't generate STCs — only PV does), incorrect postcode zone, overclaiming kW capacity</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="How do I calculate and claim STCs for a solar installation in Australia?">⚡ Ask about STC calculations</button>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Category 2: Battery Storage — AS/NZS 5139 -->
        <div class="lib-category">
          <button class="lib-cat-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="lib-cat-2">
            <span class="lib-cat-icon">🔋</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-title">Battery Storage — AS/NZS 5139</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-count">5 Q&amp;As</span>
            <svg class="lib-cat-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="lib-cat-body" id="lib-cat-2">

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-2-1" data-prompt="What are the AS/NZS 5139 clearance requirements for a Tesla Powerwall 3 installed in a garage?">
                What are the AS/NZS 5139 clearance requirements for a Tesla Powerwall?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-2-1">
                <p>AS/NZS 5139:2019 sets safety clearances for all battery energy storage systems. For a Tesla Powerwall 3 (lithium-ion, Class 1):</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>600mm clearance</strong> from any ignition source (gas appliances, pilot lights, electrical switchboards with arc-producing contacts)</li>
                  <li><strong>500mm clearance</strong> from openings to the interior of a habitable area (doors, windows, vents)</li>
                  <li><strong>Garage installations:</strong> the Powerwall 3 is rated for indoor and covered outdoor areas — confirm the specific location is compliant with the manufacturer's installation guide AND 5139</li>
                  <li><strong>Ventilation:</strong> natural ventilation is generally sufficient for Powerwall 3 in a garage; forced ventilation required in enclosed spaces</li>
                  <li><strong>Signage:</strong> AS/NZS 5139 requires battery hazard signage at or near the unit</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Always cross-reference the manufacturer's installation guide with AS/NZS 5139 — the more restrictive requirement applies. Ask Tradie Brain for a specific installation scenario for a more detailed answer.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What are the AS/NZS 5139 clearance requirements for a Tesla Powerwall 3 installed in a garage?">⚡ Ask about Powerwall clearances</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-2-2" data-prompt="Can I install a battery storage system inside a house in Australia under AS/NZS 5139?">
                Can I install a battery inside the house (not the garage)?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-2-2">
                <p>Yes, but with strict conditions under AS/NZS 5139. An indoor installation in a <strong>habitable space</strong> (living room, bedroom, hallway) requires:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>The battery must be installed in an <strong>enclosure</strong> with appropriate fire and thermal runaway protection</li>
                  <li>The enclosure must have <strong>external venting to outside</strong> — not into the roof void or wall cavity</li>
                  <li>The battery must be a <strong>Class 1 system</strong> (as defined by 5139) with all required protection devices</li>
                  <li>A minimum of <strong>600mm clearance</strong> from ignition sources</li>
                  <li>Manufacturer must explicitly approve the indoor application</li>
                </ul>
                <p>In practice, most residential batteries are installed in garages or externally on a wall. Inside-the-house installations require careful documentation and are scrutinised more closely in CER audits.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="Can I install a battery storage system inside a house in Australia under AS/NZS 5139?">⚡ Ask about indoor battery installs</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-2-3" data-prompt="What signage is required for a battery storage system installation under AS/NZS 5139 in Australia?">
                What signage is required for a battery storage installation?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-2-3">
                <p>AS/NZS 5139 requires the following signage for battery installations:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Battery energy storage system hazard label</strong> — at or near the unit, warning of electrical and fire hazards</li>
                  <li><strong>Isolation label</strong> — showing location and operating procedure for the main isolation switch</li>
                  <li><strong>Emergency contacts label</strong> — where required by state regulations (VIC, NSW)</li>
                  <li><strong>No smoking / no naked flame signs</strong> — in enclosed installations with venting</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Missing or incorrect signage is one of the top 5 CER audit findings for battery installs. Check the CEC's published Battery Installation Guide for current approved label designs.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What signage is required for a battery storage system installation under AS/NZS 5139 in Australia?">⚡ Ask about battery signage</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-2-4" data-prompt="What documentation do I need to keep for a battery storage installation for CER audit purposes?">
                What documentation do I need for a battery storage installation?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-2-4">
                <p>For each battery installation, retain:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Site-specific wiring diagram</strong> — showing battery, inverter/charger, switchboard connection, isolation, and protection devices</li>
                  <li><strong>AS/NZS 5139 clearance checklist</strong> — documented measurements and compliance confirmation</li>
                  <li><strong>Manufacturer's installation guide</strong> — version used at time of install</li>
                  <li><strong>Commissioning record</strong> — with SOC (state of charge) at handover, BMS confirmation, any error codes cleared</li>
                  <li><strong>Photos</strong> — unit installed, signage in place, clearances visible, switchboard connection</li>
                  <li><strong>Customer sign-off</strong> — acknowledging the system location, emergency procedure, and isolation point</li>
                  <li><strong>Electrical safety certificate</strong> (CES in VIC, CCEW in NSW, equivalent in other states)</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What documentation do I need to keep for a battery storage installation for CER audit purposes?">⚡ Ask about battery documentation</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-2-5" data-prompt="What are the differences between the SA Home Battery Scheme, VIC Solar Homes battery rebate, and federal battery incentives in 2026?">
                What state battery rebates are available in Australia in 2026?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-2-5">
                <p>Current battery rebate programs in Australia (2026):</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>SA — Home Battery Scheme:</strong> Up to $3,000 subsidy for eligible SA households, means-tested. Requires CEC-accredited installer and approved battery. Application via the SA Government portal.</li>
                  <li><strong>VIC — Solar Homes Battery Rebate:</strong> Up to $2,950 rebate plus optional interest-free loan for battery storage. Combined with solar or standalone. Must use a Solar Victoria registered installer.</li>
                  <li><strong>QLD — Battery Booster:</strong> $4,000 subsidy for eligible Queensland households including renters and owners. Part of the QLD Energy and Jobs Plan.</li>
                  <li><strong>Federal — Small Battery Incentive:</strong> Subject to 2025 federal budget announcements — check current CER guidance for the latest rules.</li>
                  <li><strong>NSW, WA, TAS, ACT, NT:</strong> No standalone residential battery rebate programs as of mid-2026; check state government websites for updates.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Eligibility, rebate amounts, and participating installer requirements change regularly. Always verify current rules before quoting a customer on rebate value.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What are the differences between the SA Home Battery Scheme, VIC Solar Homes battery rebate, and federal battery incentives in 2026?">⚡ Ask about battery rebates</button>
