        <!-- Category 3: Solar PV — AS/NZS 5033 -->
        <div class="lib-category">
          <button class="lib-cat-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="lib-cat-3">
            <span class="lib-cat-icon">☀️</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-title">Solar PV — AS/NZS 5033</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-count">5 Q&amp;As</span>
            <svg class="lib-cat-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="lib-cat-body" id="lib-cat-3">

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-3-1" data-prompt="What are the AS/NZS 5033 labelling requirements for a residential solar installation in Australia?">
                What are the AS/NZS 5033 labelling requirements for a residential solar install?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-3-1">
                <p>AS/NZS 5033:2021 requires labels at multiple points on every installation:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Array/rooftop:</strong> "CAUTION — Solar Array" label on each sub-array and combiner box; string polarity labels on each string</li>
                  <li><strong>Inverter:</strong> "Solar Supply Main Switch" at the AC isolator; inverter label must show rated output and serial number</li>
                  <li><strong>Main switchboard:</strong> "SOLAR SUPPLY — DO NOT SWITCH OFF" or equivalent warning label on the inverter circuit breaker</li>
                  <li><strong>DC isolator:</strong> "DC Isolator — PV Array" with warning that terminals may remain live after isolation</li>
                  <li><strong>Meter board (where applicable):</strong> "SOLAR GRID CONNECT SYSTEM INSTALLED" with system capacity and installer details</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Missing labels are one of the most common CER audit failures. Use a labelling checklist as part of your post-install sign-off.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What are the AS/NZS 5033 labelling requirements for a residential solar installation in Australia?">⚡ Ask about 5033 labelling</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-3-2" data-prompt="What is the 1.5 kW export limit under the CSIP-AUS standard and how do I comply when installing solar in Australia?">
                What is the 1.5 kW export limit and how do I set it up?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-3-2">
                <p>The <strong>1.5 kW default export limit</strong> applies under the CSIP-AUS (Common Smart Inverter Profile) framework that most Australian DNSPs are adopting from 2024 onward.</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>New inverters must support CSIP-AUS or be set to a 1.5 kW export limit at commissioning in networks that have adopted this requirement</li>
                  <li>Configuration is done via the inverter's communication port (Wi-Fi, RS485, or CSIP-AUS gateway) — process varies by brand (Fronius, Sungrow, SMA, Enphase etc.)</li>
                  <li>Confirm with the DNSP before install whether the 1.5 kW default applies to the specific site</li>
                  <li>Higher export limits (above 1.5 kW) require a DNSP application and approval</li>
                  <li>Documentation: record the export limit setting in your commissioning report</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What is the 1.5 kW export limit under the CSIP-AUS standard and how do I comply when installing solar in Australia?">⚡ Ask about export limits</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-3-3" data-prompt="What are the overcurrent protection requirements for a solar string inverter under AS/NZS 5033?">
                What overcurrent protection is required for a string inverter under AS/NZS 5033?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-3-3">
                <p>AS/NZS 5033:2021 requires overcurrent protection on the DC side where the short-circuit current of parallel strings exceeds the rating of the cables or inverter inputs:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Single string:</strong> typically no string fuse required if the cable is rated for the short-circuit current of the string</li>
                  <li><strong>Two parallel strings or more:</strong> string fuses or combiner box with fusing required for each string where the parallel fault current exceeds cable rating</li>
                  <li><strong>Fuse rating:</strong> must not exceed 2 × Isc of the string (check manufacturer's datasheet)</li>
                  <li><strong>AC side:</strong> circuit breaker at the switchboard sized to the inverter's rated AC output</li>
                  <li><strong>DC isolator:</strong> required between the array and inverter — must be DC-rated, not AC-rated</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What are the overcurrent protection requirements for a solar string inverter under AS/NZS 5033?">⚡ Ask about overcurrent protection</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-3-4" data-prompt="What wiring diagram must I produce for a solar installation to satisfy AS/NZS 5033 and the CER?">
                What must a compliant solar wiring diagram include?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-3-4">
                <p>A CER-compliant, site-specific wiring diagram must include:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Array layout — number of panels, strings, string configuration (series × parallel)</li>
                  <li>All DC cable sizes and ratings</li>
                  <li>DC overcurrent protection (string fuses, if applicable)</li>
                  <li>DC isolator location and specification</li>
                  <li>Inverter make, model, and serial number</li>
                  <li>AC isolator between inverter and switchboard</li>
                  <li>Circuit breaker rating at main switchboard</li>
                  <li>Export limit setting (if applicable)</li>
                  <li>Battery connection (if applicable) with AS/NZS 5139 clearance note</li>
                  <li>Installer name, accreditation number, installation date, and site address</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Generic or template diagrams fail CER audits. The diagram must reflect what is actually installed at that specific site.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What wiring diagram must I produce for a solar installation to satisfy AS/NZS 5033 and the CER?">⚡ Ask about wiring diagrams</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-3-5" data-prompt="What does CEC accreditation require to stay current as a solar installer in Australia?">
                What do I need to do to keep my CEC accreditation current?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-3-5">
                <p>CEC (Clean Energy Council) accreditation renewal requires:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Annual renewal</strong> — pay the annual accreditation fee by the expiry date (non-payment = immediate lapse)</li>
                  <li><strong>Professional Development:</strong> complete the required CPD hours each renewal period (check CEC portal for current requirements)</li>
                  <li><strong>Standards updates:</strong> if AS/NZS 5033 or 5139 have been updated, you may need to complete an online update module</li>
                  <li><strong>Licence check:</strong> your state electrical licence must remain current — CEC will check this at renewal</li>
                  <li><strong>Insurance:</strong> public liability and professional indemnity insurance must be current</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Letting your CEC accreditation lapse means you cannot create STCs, which effectively halts your ability to sell rebate-included solar to customers.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What does CEC accreditation require to stay current as a solar installer in Australia?">⚡ Ask about CEC accreditation</button>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Category 4: EV Chargers & Switchboards -->
        <div class="lib-category">
          <button class="lib-cat-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="lib-cat-4">
            <span class="lib-cat-icon">⚡</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-title">EV Chargers &amp; Switchboards</span>
            <span class="lib-cat-count">5 Q&amp;As</span>
            <svg class="lib-cat-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
