          <div class="lib-cat-body" id="lib-cat-4">

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-4-1" data-prompt="What DNSP approval or notification do I need to install a 7.2 kW EV charger at a residential property in NSW?">
                What DNSP approval do I need for a residential EV charger in NSW?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-4-1">
                <p>For a <strong>7.2 kW (32A) single-phase EV charger</strong> at a residential property in NSW:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Ausgrid:</strong> No prior approval required for chargers up to 7.2 kW single-phase — notification via the Ausgrid online portal after installation</li>
                  <li><strong>Endeavour Energy:</strong> No pre-approval required for up to 7.2 kW — notify after installation</li>
                  <li><strong>Essential Energy:</strong> May require a pre-installation request for larger rural connections — check Essential Energy's website for the specific feeder</li>
                  <li><strong>All NSW DNSPs:</strong> For three-phase or chargers above 7.2 kW, a network application is required before installation</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Always check the DNSP's current EV charger requirements — they update frequently as EV uptake increases. Include DNSP notification confirmation in your job documentation.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What DNSP approval or notification do I need to install a 7.2 kW EV charger at a residential property in NSW?">⚡ Ask about EV charger DNSP approval</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-4-2" data-prompt="When does a solar or EV charger installation require a switchboard upgrade under AS/NZS 3000?">
                When does a solar or EV install require a switchboard upgrade?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-4-2">
                <p>AS/NZS 3000 requires you to upgrade a switchboard when the new work creates a non-compliant condition:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>No RCD protection:</strong> If the existing switchboard has no RCDs on power circuits (old-style fuse boards), adding a solar or EV circuit triggers an upgrade requirement</li>
                  <li><strong>Insufficient capacity:</strong> If the main switch/fuse is undersized for the combined load after adding the EV charger or solar inverter AC output</li>
                  <li><strong>No spare circuit breaker position:</strong> Cannot add a new circuit without physical space in the board</li>
                  <li><strong>Existing non-compliant wiring:</strong> If you disturb or extend wiring that doesn't meet current standards, you must bring that work up to AS/NZS 3000</li>
                </ul>
                <p>This is the most common unexpected cost in EV charger and solar quotes. Always assess the switchboard in your initial site inspection and include a switchboard upgrade allowance in your quote if the board is pre-2000.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="When does a solar or EV charger installation require a switchboard upgrade under AS/NZS 3000?">⚡ Ask about switchboard upgrade triggers</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-4-3" data-prompt="What load management options are available for a commercial EV charger installation in Australia?">
                What are the load management options for a commercial EV charger install?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-4-3">
                <p>For a commercial site with multiple EV chargers, load management is critical to avoid exceeding the site's supply capacity:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Static load sharing:</strong> Maximum combined output capped regardless of actual demand — simple, low cost</li>
                  <li><strong>Dynamic load management (DLM):</strong> Chargers communicate with each other and a load controller to distribute available capacity in real time, prioritising active vehicles</li>
                  <li><strong>OCPP-based management:</strong> Open Charge Point Protocol allows a back-end management platform to control individual charger output — best for multi-site or fleet applications</li>
                  <li><strong>CSIP-AUS integration:</strong> Some commercial sites require chargers that can respond to DNSP signals to reduce export/import — particularly in areas with constrained networks</li>
                </ul>
                <p>For commercial installs above 22 kW total, a formal load study and DNSP application is typically required. Factor design and DNSP approval time into your project timeline.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What load management options are available for a commercial EV charger installation in Australia?">⚡ Ask about commercial EV load management</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-4-4" data-prompt="How do I quote an EV charger installation job for a residential customer including all potential costs?">
                How do I quote an EV charger installation so I don't get caught out on costs?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-4-4">
                <p>A complete EV charger quote should cover:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Charger hardware:</strong> AC Level 2 (7.2 kW typical) or DC fast charger — confirm customer's vehicle compatibility</li>
                  <li><strong>Installation labour:</strong> cable run from switchboard to garage — quote per-metre for longer runs, not a flat rate</li>
                  <li><strong>Switchboard assessment:</strong> Include a conditional line item for upgrade if the board is pre-2000; don't absorb this cost</li>
                  <li><strong>DNSP notification fee:</strong> Low cost but required; include time for follow-up</li>
                  <li><strong>Trenching/conduit:</strong> If the cable run goes underground or through walls</li>
                  <li><strong>Permit/CCEW/CES fee:</strong> Your state electrical certificate cost</li>
                  <li><strong>Smart charging setup:</strong> If the customer wants app-based or solar-optimised charging — allow for commissioning time</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Quote with allowances and communicate clearly. More EV charger jobs blow out on cable run length and switchboard surprises than on the charger itself.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="How do I quote an EV charger installation job for a residential customer including all potential costs?">⚡ Ask about EV charger quoting</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-4-5" data-prompt="What is the difference between a Type 1 and Type 2 EV charger connector in Australia?">
                What's the difference between Type 1 and Type 2 EV connectors?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-4-5">
                <p>The two main AC charging connector standards in Australia:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Type 1 (SAE J1772):</strong> 5-pin, single-phase only, max 7.2 kW. Used on older EVs including early Nissan Leaf, Mitsubishi Outlander PHEV (pre-2019). Being phased out in new vehicles.</li>
                  <li><strong>Type 2 (IEC 62196-2 / Mennekes):</strong> 7-pin, supports single-phase (7.2 kW) and three-phase (22 kW). Standard on virtually all new EVs sold in Australia from 2020 onwards — Tesla, Hyundai Ioniq, BYD, MG ZS EV, Kia EV6, etc.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>For new installations:</strong> always install a Type 2 charger. Many quality chargers include both a tethered Type 2 cable and a Type 2 socket so customers with different vehicles can use the charger. Confirm the customer's vehicle inlet type before quoting — some PHEVs still have Type 1.</p>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="What is the difference between a Type 1 and Type 2 EV charger connector in Australia?">⚡ Ask about EV connector types</button>
              </div>
