              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-6-1" data-prompt="How should I price a residential solar and battery installation for a good margin in Australia in 2026?">
                How do I price a solar + battery job for a good margin?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-6-1">
                <p>Solar and battery pricing in Australia has compressed significantly. To protect your margin:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Know your cost base:</strong> Materials + labour + overheads + compliance costs + warranty provision — most small operators underestimate overheads by 15–25%</li>
                  <li><strong>Target 25–35% gross margin</strong> on materials and 50–60% gross margin on labour</li>
                  <li><strong>Don't compete on price with the volume stackers</strong> — compete on quality, documentation, and compliance. The market is splitting between cheap-and-risky and quality-and-documented.</li>
                  <li><strong>3-tier quoting:</strong> present a Good / Better / Best option — customers who would have bought Good often upgrade to Better when they see the comparison</li>
                  <li><strong>Price the risk separately:</strong> switchboard upgrade, difficult roof access, battery in a non-standard location — itemise these so the customer sees the value, not just the total</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="How should I price a residential solar and battery installation for a good margin in Australia in 2026?">⚡ Ask about solar pricing strategy</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-6-2" data-prompt="When should a solar installation business hire their first full-time employee or apprentice in Australia?">
                When should I hire my first employee or apprentice?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-6-2">
                <p>The right time to hire is when you are consistently turning down work or when your admin load is preventing you from doing revenue-generating work:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Financial trigger:</strong> 3+ months of consistent revenue above your cost base + new hire cost. Don't hire on a spike.</li>
                  <li><strong>Apprentice vs sub:</strong> Apprentice = lower immediate cost but significant time investment in training and supervision. Subcontractor = higher per-job cost but flexible and faster to deploy. Most solo operators start with a reliable sub, then apprentice when volume sustains it.</li>
                  <li><strong>Award rates (2026):</strong> Electrical apprentices are covered by the Electrical, Electronic and Communications Contracting Award 2020 — check Fair Work Australia for current rates</li>
                  <li><strong>Compliance:</strong> Hiring an apprentice requires registration with your state training authority (e.g. TAFE NSW, VIC Skills First) and a formal training contract</li>
                  <li><strong>ServiceM8 impact:</strong> Before hiring, build your SOPs and job templates so the new person can follow your system — don't hire and then build the system</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="When should a solar installation business hire their first full-time employee or apprentice in Australia?">⚡ Ask about hiring your first employee</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-6-3" data-prompt="How do I manage cash flow as a solar installer dealing with large material costs and slow-paying customers?">
                How do I manage cash flow when material costs are high?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-6-3">
                <p>Solar installation cash flow is brutally front-loaded — you buy equipment weeks before you get paid. Fixes:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Require a deposit:</strong> 30–50% upfront before ordering equipment. Non-negotiable. Automate this with ServiceM8 + Stripe.</li>
                  <li><strong>Negotiate supplier terms:</strong> 30-day or 60-day supplier credit means you can complete and invoice before your materials cost comes due</li>
                  <li><strong>Invoice on completion, not monthly:</strong> Same-day invoicing after every job. Every day you wait is a day of free credit to your customer.</li>
                  <li><strong>7-day payment terms:</strong> Not 30 days. Residential customers don't need 30 days. Make it 7 days and follow up on day 8.</li>
                  <li><strong>Maintain a cash buffer:</strong> 6–8 weeks of operating costs in a separate account — treat it as untouchable except for genuine emergencies</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="How do I manage cash flow as a solar installer dealing with large material costs and slow-paying customers?">⚡ Ask about solar business cash flow</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-6-4" data-prompt="How do I win commercial solar contracts in Australia as a small to medium solar installer?">
                How do I win commercial solar contracts as a smaller installer?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-6-4">
                <p>Commercial solar is different territory — the margins are better but the qualification bar is higher:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Credentials first:</strong> You need public liability insurance of at least $10M, professional indemnity, and ideally a history of completed commercial installs. Without these you won't get past the tender questionnaire.</li>
                  <li><strong>Target the right size:</strong> 30–100 kW commercial jobs are the sweet spot for small-medium installers. The big EPCs dominate MW-scale; you can win on service and responsiveness at the SME level.</li>
                  <li><strong>Know the procurement process:</strong> Most commercial clients use a 3-quote process. Your quote needs a professional design report, energy modelling (Helioscope or similar), ROI analysis, and a clear project schedule.</li>
                  <li><strong>References matter more than price:</strong> One strong reference from a local business owner is worth more than a 5% lower price in commercial tendering</li>
                  <li><strong>AS/NZS 5033 commercial design compliance:</strong> String sizing, combiner boxes, surge protection, and metering requirements are more complex at commercial scale — make sure your design is watertight before submitting</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="How do I win commercial solar contracts in Australia as a small to medium solar installer?">⚡ Ask about winning commercial solar</button>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lib-qa">
              <button class="lib-qa-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="qa-6-5" data-prompt="How do I scale a solar installation business from 1 to 3 crews without losing quality or compliance?">
                How do I scale from 1 to 3 install crews without losing quality?
                <svg class="lib-qa-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
              </button>
              <div class="lib-qa-body" id="qa-6-5">
                <p>Scaling is where most solar businesses lose control of compliance and quality. The sequence that works:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Step 1 — Systemise before you scale:</strong> Every job process must be documented and running in ServiceM8 with checklists, forms, and photo requirements before you add crew number 2</li>
                  <li><strong>Step 2 — Prove the system on crew 2 first:</strong> Don't add crew 3 until crew 2 can complete a job end-to-end, including compliance documentation, without you in the field</li>
                  <li><strong>Step 3 — Separate sales from operations:</strong> You (or someone you hire) needs to own sales. The install crews need to own delivery. When the owner is doing both, growth stalls at the bottleneck.</li>
                  <li><strong>Step 4 — CER audit-proof each crew:</strong> Every crew member must know the compliance non-negotiables: consent before STC, site-specific wiring diagram, all labelling, photos before the truck leaves</li>
                  <li><strong>Step 5 — Review and fix:</strong> Monthly QA audits on a random sample of each crew's jobs. Catch compliance drift before the CER does.</li>
                </ul>
                <button class="lib-ask-btn" data-prompt="How do I scale a solar installation business from 1 to 3 crews without losing quality or compliance?">⚡ Ask about scaling your solar business</button>
              </div>
